        'details.metaballs.description.isosurface_text_1': 'To find the surface, the potentials of all n balls are added up at every point:',
        'details.metaballs.description.isosurface_text_2': 'A point belongs to the metaball if this sum reaches or exceeds the threshold τ. The surface itself is the isosurface on which the sum is exactly τ:',
        'details.metaballs.description.example_heading': 'An example in 2D',
        'details.metaballs.description.coordinate_separator': ',',
        'details.metaballs.description.example_text': 'To illustrate this, let us go through a simplified example in the plane step by step. First, we fix the threshold and the falloff function:',
        'details.metaballs.description.single_ball_heading': 'One metaball',
        'details.metaballs.description.single_ball_step_1': 'In the plane, every ball becomes a circle. For simplicity, we assume there is only one ball, with its centre at the origin c₁ = (0, 0). For a point p = (x, y), its distance r₁ to c₁ is therefore simply its distance to the origin; the zeros of c₁ drop out:',
        'details.metaballs.description.single_ball_step_1_potential': 'The potential of p depends only on this distance:',
        'details.metaballs.description.single_ball_step_2': 'p belongs to the ball if its potential reaches at least the threshold:',
        'details.metaballs.description.single_ball_step_3': 'We now solve this inequality for r₁ step by step. As a distance, r₁ is positive, so we may multiply both sides by r₁ without flipping the ≥:',
        'details.metaballs.description.single_ball_step_4': 'Then we divide both sides by 0.7:',
        'details.metaballs.description.single_ball_step_5': 'With r₁ = √(x² + y²) this means: all points whose distance to the origin is at most 1.43 belong to the ball. These are exactly the points of a circle around (0, 0) with the radius 1.43:',
        'details.metaballs.description.single_ball_step_6': 'Now we check the point P = (1.5, 0). First we compute the distance to c₁:',
        'details.metaballs.description.single_ball_step_7': 'With this distance, its potential is:',
        'details.metaballs.description.single_ball_step_8': 'The potential lies below the threshold. So P does not belong to the ball, but lies just outside the circle:',
        'details.metaballs.description.two_balls_heading': 'Two metaballs',
        'details.metaballs.description.two_balls_step_1': 'Now we add a second ball with its centre at c₂ = (3, 0). The potential of a point is now the sum of the contributions of both balls:',
        'details.metaballs.description.two_balls_step_2': 'We keep looking at the point P = (1.5, 0). We already know the distance from P to c₁, r₁ = 1.5. What is new is the distance to c₂:',
        'details.metaballs.description.two_balls_step_3': 'Both contributions are added up:',
        'details.metaballs.description.two_balls_step_4': 'The sum now lies above the threshold:',
        'details.metaballs.description.two_balls_conclusion': 'So P belongs to the metaball, even though the point previously lay outside the single ball: the two metaballs have merged into one shape. Since 1/⁠r falls off only slowly, this shared shape grows well beyond the two circles.',
        'details.metaballs.description.single_ball_figure_alt': 'Coordinate system with a circle around the origin; the point P at (1.5,\u00a00) lies just outside it.',
